Part V — Advanced topics: Rust ownership (2 marks). Which statements are correct?
```rust
fn main() {
    let s = String::from("abc");
    let t = s;
    let u = t.clone();
    println!("{}", t);
    println!("{}", s);
}
```

Part V — Advanced topics: Rust borrowing (2 marks). Which statements about this code are correct?
```rust
fn main() {
    let mut s = String::from("hi");

    let a = &s;
    let b = &s;
    println!("{} {}", a, b);

    let c = &mut s;
    c.push_str("!");
}
```

Part V — Advanced topics: concurrency (4 marks). Fill the concurrency concepts.
```java
// Java example: code inside a Counter.inc() method.
// The shared field is: int count = 0;

// Looks like one source-level operation, but it is not atomic.
count++;
```
The operation `count++` is really a read-modify-write sequence: first {step1} the old value, then {step2} the new value, then {step3} it back. If two threads do this without coordination and at least one access writes the shared variable, the bug is a {bug}.

